From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 20:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] parser: accept static and extern as storage-class specifiers

The lexer listed "static" and "extern" in c99_reserved, and the reserved-word
check runs before the keyword lookup, so any use of them was rejected with
"'static' is reserved".

Both are already mapped to the STATIC/EXTERN tokens a few lines below, and
__declaration_specifiers() already parses (and ignores) them, so removing
them from the reserved set is all that is needed.

OpenCL C 1.2 permits both on program-scope declarations, and kernels in the
wild rely on it -- hashcat's DECLSPEC expands to static on every one of its
device functions, so no hashcat kernel could be parsed at all.
---
--- a/src/parser/lex.cpp
+++ b/src/parser/lex.cpp
@@ -272,8 +272,11 @@
 						reserved.insert("double" + suffix);
 					}
 				}
-				const char *c99_reserved[] = {"auto", "register", "static",
-											  "entry", "extern" };
+				// NB: "static" and "extern" are NOT reserved here: OpenCL C code
+				// (e.g. hashcat kernels) uses them as function storage-class
+				// specifiers. They are mapped to STATIC/EXTERN keywords below and
+				// consumed (and ignored) by __declaration_specifiers().
+				const char *c99_reserved[] = {"auto", "register", "entry" };
 				reserved.insert(c99_reserved, c99_reserved + sizeof(c99_reserved) / sizeof(*c99_reserved));
 
 				keywords["typedef"] = TYPEDEF;
